window.registerTopic('react-adv', {
  why: `Once the basics are confirmed, interviewers test whether you can **structure** a React application: share logic with hooks, HOCs or render props; share data with context; render outside the tree with portals; recover from errors with boundaries; and keep it fast with memoisation, lazy loading and code splitting. They also probe hooks rules and the differences between \`useEffect\` and \`useLayoutEffect\`, \`useState\` and \`useRef\`.

These answers should include *when not to* use each tool: context re-renders every consumer, HOCs add wrapper layers, memo only helps when props are stable.`,
  notes: [
    { h: 'Hooks', md: `**Hooks** (React 16.8) are functions that let function components use state and other React features without classes, isolating stateful logic for reuse. \`useState\` returns \`[value, setter]\` (array destructuring lets you name them freely); \`useEffect\` runs side effects after paint, with a dependency array controlling when (empty array = once on mount; return a cleanup function for unsubscribing or clearing timers); \`useLayoutEffect\` runs synchronously after DOM mutation but before paint, for measuring layout or avoiding flicker; \`useRef\` holds a mutable \`.current\` that persists across renders **without causing re-renders**, for DOM nodes, timers and previous values; \`useContext\`, \`useReducer\`, \`useMemo\`, \`useCallback\` round out the core.

Two **rules**: call hooks only at the top level (not in loops, conditions or nested functions) so their order is stable, and only from React function components or custom hooks. \`eslint-plugin-react-hooks\` enforces both (it treats any function starting with \`use\` + capital letter as a hook). Hooks replace most render props and HOCs with less nesting, but they do not cover \`getSnapshotBeforeUpdate\` or \`componentDidCatch\`, so error boundaries remain classes. You need not rewrite existing classes; adopt hooks in new components. Fetch data in \`useEffect\` and store it with \`useState\`; subscribe to resize with an effect that adds and removes the listener.

\`\`\`jsx
function useWindowWidth() {
  const [w, setW] = useState(window.innerWidth);
  useEffect(() => {
    const onResize = () => setW(window.innerWidth);
    window.addEventListener("resize", onResize);
    return () => window.removeEventListener("resize", onResize);
  }, []);
  return w;
}
\`\`\`` },
    { h: 'Context', md: `**Context** passes data through the tree without threading props at every level: themes, locale, authenticated user. \`const ThemeContext = React.createContext(defaultValue)\`; the **Provider** supplies a value; consumers read it via \`useContext\`, the \`<Consumer>\` render-prop component, or \`static contextType\` in classes. The \`defaultValue\` is used only when no Provider is above (handy for tests). Context compares the value by **reference identity**, so an inline object literal in \`value={{…}}\` re-renders every consumer on each parent render; memoise the value or lift it into state. Context vs Redux: context is a transport for dependency injection; Redux adds a predictable store, middleware, DevTools and selectors. For persisting auth on refresh with context, run a "load user" effect in the top-level provider.` },
    { h: 'Composition patterns: HOCs, render props, children', md: `React favours **composition over inheritance**. A **higher-order component** is a function that takes a component and returns an enhanced one (\`withRouter\`, \`connect\`). Implementations: **props proxy** (wrap and add/edit props, read instance via ref, abstract state) and **inheritance inversion** (extend the wrapped component to hijack \`render\`). Caveats: do not create HOCs inside \`render\` (new type each time remounts the subtree), copy over statics, forward refs with \`React.forwardRef\` because \`ref\` is not a prop, and avoid wrapper hell. \`React.forwardRef\` components receive \`(props, ref)\`; ordinary components never see \`ref\`; adopting forwardRef in a library is a breaking change. Name the render function for DevTools.

**Render props** share code through a prop whose value is a function returning elements (\`<Mouse render={pos => …} />\`); the prop need not be called \`render\`, and \`children\` as a function is the same idea. Beware with PureComponent: an inline render function defeats shallow comparison; define it as an instance method. **Render hijacking** is controlling another component's output by wrapping it. **Mixins** are the deprecated predecessor; decorators (\`@connect\`) are sugar for applying HOCs. **Wrapper components** add layout, styling or behaviour around children.` },
    { h: 'Fragments, portals and conditional output', md: `**Fragments** (\`<></>\` or \`<React.Fragment key>\`) group children without an extra DOM node: faster and lighter in deep trees, valid inside tables and flex/grid layouts where a stray div breaks CSS, and the only Fragment attribute is \`key\`. Since React 16 \`render\` may return arrays, strings and numbers too.

**Portals** (\`ReactDOM.createPortal(child, domNode)\`) render children into a DOM node outside the parent hierarchy while keeping React context and event bubbling; use them for modals, tooltips and dropdowns that must escape \`overflow: hidden\` or stacking contexts. Return \`null\` to render nothing.` },
    { h: 'Error boundaries', md: `An **error boundary** is a class component that defines \`static getDerivedStateFromError(error)\` (return state to show a fallback) and/or \`componentDidCatch(error, info)\` (log; \`info.componentStack\` has the component stack trace with file names). It catches errors thrown during rendering, in lifecycle methods and in constructors of the subtree below it. It does **not** catch errors in event handlers (use try/catch; they do not happen during render), asynchronous code (setTimeout, requestAnimationFrame), server-side rendering, or errors thrown by the boundary itself. Place boundaries at route level for a generic message and around individual widgets to keep the rest alive. In React 16 an uncaught error unmounts the whole tree. React 15's \`unstable_handleError\` was the precursor. Third-party \`react-error-boundary\` avoids writing the class yourself.` },
    { h: 'Performance: memo, lazy, Suspense, code splitting, windowing', md: `\`React.memo(Component, areEqual?)\` skips re-rendering a function component when props are shallow-equal (classes use \`PureComponent\` or \`shouldComponentUpdate\`; a \`setState\` updater can return \`null\` to skip). Inline arrow functions and object literals create new props every render and defeat memoisation; use \`useCallback\`/\`useMemo\` or class fields. Rate-limit expensive handlers with throttling, debouncing or \`requestAnimationFrame\`.

**Code splitting** lets bundlers emit chunks loaded on demand via dynamic \`import()\`. \`React.lazy(() => import('./Other'))\` renders a dynamic import as a component (default exports only; re-export named exports through an intermediate module) and must be wrapped in \`<Suspense fallback={…}>\`. **Route-based splitting** wraps each route component; loadable-components covers server rendering, which \`lazy\` did not. **Windowing** (react-window, react-virtualized) renders only the visible rows of long lists.

**Concurrent rendering** (formerly "async mode") lets React interrupt long renders to handle urgent updates, keeping the UI responsive. **Strict mode** (\`<React.StrictMode>\`) is a development-only wrapper that double-invokes render and effects to surface impure logic, and warns about unsafe lifecycles, string refs, legacy context and findDOMNode. **React Server Components** render on the server and stream to the client with zero bundle cost. The **new JSX transform** (React 17) removes the need to import React in every file and can shrink bundles slightly. **Imperative vs declarative**: you describe the desired UI for a given state and React works out the DOM operations.` },
    { h: 'Odds and ends', md: `\`React.Children\` helpers and \`cloneElement\` (clone an element with new props) support composition; \`createElement\` builds elements. Decorators are a Babel stage proposal. Web components can be used inside React, and React components inside web components; access their imperative APIs via refs. Avoid \`javascript:\` URLs (warned since 16.9). Use \`displayName\` and named forwardRef render functions for DevTools. Prefer composition to inheritance for reuse between components.` },
  ],
  pitfalls: [
    'Calling a hook inside a condition or loop. Hook order must be identical on every render.',
    'Forgetting the cleanup function in useEffect for subscriptions and timers, which leaks and causes "setState on unmounted component".',
    'Passing a fresh object literal as a context value and re-rendering every consumer on each render.',
    'Expecting an error boundary to catch errors in click handlers or setTimeout callbacks.',
    'Wrapping everything in React.memo while still passing new inline callbacks, so nothing is actually skipped.',
    'Defining a HOC or component inside render, which remounts the subtree every time.',
  ],
  model: [
    { q: 'What are the rules of hooks and why do they exist?', a: 'Only call hooks at the top level of a function component or custom hook, never inside loops, conditions or nested functions, and only call them from React functions. React identifies each hook by its call order within a render, so the sequence must be identical every time; a conditional hook would shift the order and corrupt state. The eslint-plugin-react-hooks package enforces both rules automatically.', ref: 'react-227' },
    { q: 'useEffect vs useLayoutEffect?', a: 'Both run after the DOM is updated. useEffect runs asynchronously after the browser has painted, so it never blocks rendering and suits data fetching, subscriptions and logging. useLayoutEffect runs synchronously before paint, so it can measure layout or adjust the DOM without a visible flicker, at the cost of delaying the frame. Default to useEffect and reach for useLayoutEffect only for measurement-driven updates.', ref: 'react-334' },
    { q: 'What problems do error boundaries solve and what are their limits?', a: 'They catch JavaScript errors thrown while rendering, in lifecycle methods or in constructors anywhere in their subtree, log them and render a fallback UI instead of unmounting the entire application, which is what React 16 does for uncaught errors. They are class components implementing getDerivedStateFromError and/or componentDidCatch. They do not catch errors in event handlers, asynchronous callbacks, server-side rendering, or in the boundary itself, so those still need try/catch.', ref: 'react-54' },
    { q: 'How would you reduce bundle size and improve load time in a React app?', a: 'Split code with dynamic import and React.lazy plus Suspense, ideally per route; load heavy or rarely used features on demand; keep lists virtualised with windowing; memoise expensive components and stable callbacks; enable production mode so PropTypes and warnings are stripped; and analyse the bundle to remove unused dependencies, which tree shaking can only do for ES-module imports.', ref: 'react-250' },
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Which hook holds a mutable value that does not trigger re-renders when changed?', options: ['useState', 'useRef', 'useMemo', 'useReducer'], answer: 1, explain: 'useRef\'s .current persists across renders silently; useState schedules a re-render.', ref: 'react-332' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Which statement violates the rules of hooks?', options: ['Calling useState at the top of a component', 'Calling useEffect inside an `if` block', 'Calling a custom hook from another custom hook', 'Calling useContext in a component'], answer: 1, explain: 'Conditional hooks change call order between renders.', ref: 'react-227' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'When is a context\'s defaultValue used?', options: ['Always', 'When the Provider value is undefined', 'Only when no matching Provider exists above the consumer', 'Never'], answer: 2, explain: 'Useful for testing components in isolation.', ref: 'react-278' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Why do inline object literals as context values cause performance problems?', options: ['Objects are not allowed as values', 'Context uses reference identity, so a new object re-renders all consumers every render', 'They break JSX parsing', 'They are frozen'], answer: 1, explain: 'Memoise the value or keep it in state.', ref: 'react-281' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Which error is NOT caught by an error boundary?', options: ['An error thrown in a child\'s render', 'An error thrown in a child\'s constructor', 'An error thrown in a click handler', 'An error thrown in componentDidMount'], answer: 2, explain: 'Event handlers do not run during rendering; use try/catch there.', ref: 'react-232' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'What must wrap a component created with React.lazy?', options: ['React.StrictMode', 'Suspense with a fallback', 'An error boundary', 'A Fragment'], answer: 1, explain: 'Suspense shows the fallback while the chunk loads.', ref: 'react-275' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'React.lazy supports…', options: ['Named exports directly', 'Only default exports (re-export named ones via an intermediate module)', 'CSS files', 'Class components only'], answer: 1, explain: 'The module must have a default export resolving to the component.', ref: 'react-44' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'What does a portal preserve even though it renders elsewhere in the DOM?', options: ['Nothing', 'React context and synthetic event bubbling through the React tree', 'Only CSS', 'The DOM parent'], answer: 1, explain: 'Portals change DOM placement, not the React tree.', ref: 'react-48' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Why are fragments better than wrapper divs?', options: ['They render faster CSS', 'They avoid extra DOM nodes, keep table/flex/grid layouts valid, and reduce memory', 'They support more attributes', 'They can hold state'], answer: 1, explain: 'key is the only attribute a Fragment accepts.', ref: 'react-47' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'In development, StrictMode…', options: ['Makes the app faster', 'Double-invokes render and effects to surface side effects and warns about unsafe APIs', 'Disables hooks', 'Enables SSR'], answer: 1, explain: 'It has no effect in production builds.', ref: 'react-337' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Which pattern is "a function that takes a component and returns a new component"?', options: ['Render prop', 'Higher-order component', 'Portal', 'Hook'], answer: 1, explain: 'Examples: connect from react-redux, withRouter.', ref: 'react-35' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Why do refs not pass through HOCs automatically?', options: ['Refs are deprecated', '`ref` is not a prop; it is handled specially like `key`, so you need React.forwardRef', 'HOCs strip all props', 'Refs only work on DOM nodes'], answer: 1, explain: 'Without forwardRef the ref points at the wrapper.', ref: 'react-282' },
    { type: 'short', q: 'What is a custom hook and what makes it one?', a: 'A function whose name starts with `use` and that calls other hooks. It lets you extract stateful logic (subscriptions, fetching, form handling) and reuse it across components without wrappers; each component calling it gets its own isolated state.', ref: 'react-226' },
    { type: 'short', q: 'Compare HOCs, render props and hooks for sharing logic.', a: 'HOCs wrap a component and inject props: reusable but add wrapper layers, hide prop origins and need ref forwarding. Render props pass a function that receives data and returns UI: explicit but nest deeply and can defeat PureComponent. Hooks extract logic into functions called from the component: no extra nesting, composable, but only in function components.', ref: 'react-78' },
    { type: 'short', q: 'What is windowing and when do you need it?', a: 'Rendering only the rows currently visible in a scrollable list (plus a small buffer) and recycling DOM nodes as the user scrolls. Libraries such as react-window implement it. Use it for lists of hundreds or thousands of items where rendering everything causes slow renders and huge DOM trees.', ref: 'react-293' },
  ],
  cards: [
    { f: 'Hooks are…', b: 'Functions (React 16.8) that let function components use state, effects and other features; reusable stateful logic.', ref: 'react-226' },
    { f: 'Two rules of hooks', b: 'Top level only (no loops/conditions/nested fns); only from React function components or custom hooks.', ref: 'react-227' },
    { f: 'useEffect vs useLayoutEffect', b: 'useEffect: after paint, async. useLayoutEffect: before paint, synchronous; for layout measurement.', ref: 'react-334' },
    { f: 'useState vs useRef', b: 'useState re-renders on change. useRef holds .current without re-rendering; DOM nodes, timers, previous values.', ref: 'react-332' },
    { f: 'Context', b: 'createContext → Provider value → useContext/Consumer/contextType. Reference identity; defaultValue only without Provider.', ref: 'react-37' },
    { f: 'HOC', b: 'Function taking a component and returning an enhanced component (props proxy or inheritance inversion).', ref: 'react-35' },
    { f: 'HOC caveats', b: 'Not inside render, copy statics, forward refs, avoid wrapper hell.', ref: 'react-254' },
    { f: 'Render prop', b: 'A prop whose value is a function returning elements; shares logic; name need not be "render".', ref: 'react-128' },
    { f: 'Fragment', b: 'Groups children without a DOM node; <></> or <Fragment key>; key is the only attribute.', ref: 'react-46' },
    { f: 'Portal', b: 'createPortal(child, domNode): render outside the parent DOM hierarchy, keep context and bubbling; modals/tooltips.', ref: 'react-48' },
    { f: 'Error boundary methods', b: 'static getDerivedStateFromError(error) → fallback state; componentDidCatch(error, info) → log.', ref: 'react-54' },
    { f: 'Error boundaries do NOT catch', b: 'Event handlers, async callbacks (setTimeout/rAF), SSR, errors in the boundary itself.', ref: 'react-232' },
    { f: 'React.memo', b: 'HOC that skips re-render when props are shallow-equal; optional custom comparison.', ref: 'react-221' },
    { f: 'React.lazy + Suspense', b: 'lazy(() => import()) renders a dynamic import; default exports only; needs Suspense fallback.', ref: 'react-222' },
    { f: 'Code splitting', b: 'Bundler emits chunks loaded on demand via dynamic import(); best at route level.', ref: 'react-250' },
    { f: 'Windowing', b: 'Render only visible rows of a long list (react-window).', ref: 'react-293' },
    { f: 'Strict mode', b: 'Dev-only wrapper: double-invokes render/effects, warns on unsafe lifecycles, string refs, legacy context.', ref: 'react-336' },
    { f: 'Concurrent rendering', b: 'React can interrupt and resume rendering work to keep the UI responsive (formerly async mode).', ref: 'react-319' },
    { f: 'forwardRef', b: 'Lets a component pass its ref to a child; (props, ref) signature; needed for HOCs and libraries.', ref: 'react-21' },
    { f: 'Composition over inheritance', b: 'Reuse via props, children and composition rather than extending components.', ref: 'react-271' },
  ],
  coding: [
    { id: 'use-fetch', title: 'Write a useFetch hook', prompt: 'Write `useFetch(url)` returning `{ data, error, loading }`, re-fetching when `url` changes and ignoring stale responses when the component unmounts or the url changes.', starter: '// function useFetch(url) {\n//   const [state, setState] = useState({ data: null, error: null, loading: true });\n//   useEffect(() => { ... }, [url]);\n//   return state;\n// }\nconsole.log("write it, then reveal");', solution: 'function useFetch(url) {\n  const [state, setState] = useState({ data: null, error: null, loading: true });\n  useEffect(() => {\n    let active = true;\n    setState(s => ({ ...s, loading: true }));\n    fetch(url)\n      .then(r => { if (!r.ok) throw new Error(r.status); return r.json(); })\n      .then(data => active && setState({ data, error: null, loading: false }))\n      .catch(error => active && setState({ data: null, error, loading: false }));\n    return () => { active = false; };\n  }, [url]);\n  return state;\n}', notes: 'The `active` flag is the cleanup that prevents setting state after unmount or for an outdated url.', ref: 'react-303' },
    { id: 'error-boundary', title: 'Write an error boundary', prompt: 'Write a class `ErrorBoundary` that shows `props.fallback` after an error and logs the component stack.', starter: '// class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {\n//   state = { hasError: false };\n//   ...\n// }\nconsole.log("write it, then reveal");', solution: 'class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {\n  state = { hasError: false };\n  static getDerivedStateFromError() { return { hasError: true }; }\n  componentDidCatch(error, info) { console.error(error, info.componentStack); }\n  render() { return this.state.hasError ? this.props.fallback : this.props.children; }\n}\n\n// <ErrorBoundary fallback={<p>Something went wrong</p>}><Widget /></ErrorBoundary>', ref: 'react-54' },
    { id: 'with-loading', title: 'Write a withLoading HOC', prompt: 'Write `withLoading(Component)` that renders "Loading…" while `props.loading` is true and otherwise renders the wrapped component with the remaining props. Set a displayName.', starter: '// function withLoading(Wrapped) { ... }\nconsole.log("write it, then reveal");', solution: 'function withLoading(Wrapped) {\n  function WithLoading({ loading, ...rest }) {\n    return loading ? <p>Loading…</p> : <Wrapped {...rest} />;\n  }\n  WithLoading.displayName = `withLoading(${Wrapped.displayName || Wrapped.name || "Component"})`;\n  return WithLoading;\n}', notes: 'Define the HOC at module level, never inside render; forward refs with React.forwardRef if the wrapped component needs them.', ref: 'react-35' },
    { id: 'theme-context', title: 'Theme context with a memoised value', prompt: 'Create a ThemeContext with a provider holding `theme` state and a `toggle` function, memoised so consumers only re-render when the theme changes.', starter: '// const ThemeContext = createContext({ theme: "dark", toggle: () => {} });\n// function ThemeProvider({ children }) { ... }\nconsole.log("write it, then reveal");', solution: 'const ThemeContext = createContext({ theme: "dark", toggle: () => {} });\n\nfunction ThemeProvider({ children }) {\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState("dark");\n  const value = useMemo(() => ({ theme, toggle: () => setTheme(t => (t === "dark" ? "light" : "dark")) }), [theme]);\n  return <ThemeContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</ThemeContext.Provider>;\n}\n\nconst useTheme = () => useContext(ThemeContext);', ref: 'react-281' },
  ],
});
